{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEPbPN4HgFUiRFQZtgV6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've fixed both bugs in `src/app.py` and written up the causes in `ANSWER.md`. Both come from the same mistake: `hidden` is a DynamoDB reserved word, and the code used it without an alias (`#name` placeholder) in two places.\n\n**Why each bug happened:**\n1. **Prod crash:** `hide()` updated the story with `SET hidden = :h, ...`. DynamoDB rejects reserved words used bare in an update, so every Hide call failed with a ValidationException and returned a 500.\n2. **Staging feed:** `feed()` asked DynamoDB for the fields `slug, title, hidden, published_at`. The bare `hidden` caused no error; the field just came back missing on every story. So the \"skip hidden stories\" check never saw a value and let every story through, with a 200 and no log line. The manual CLI write was fine; the feed isn't cached and reads the right table. Nobody saw this earlier because the Hide button crashed first.\n\n**The fix:**\n- Every attribute name in every expression now goes through an alias, using a small `_names()` helper. The rule is \"alias always\", not \"alias words on the reserved list\", because the list has about 570 ordinary words (`status`, `name`, `date`, …).\n- `archive()` had the same bug waiting: its condition used `status` bare, which would crash the first time anything called it. Fixed the same way.\n- `feed()` now reads every page of the table scan instead of only the first 1 MB.\n- Method names and signatures are unchanged, so `api.py` doesn't need to change.\n\n**So it can't come back:** the tests now run against a stricter fake table that fails any call with an unaliased attribute name. The existing fake only knows some of the reserved words, so a new field could otherwise pass the tests and fail on AWS. I also added tests for hiding through the app, a story hidden by hand like Dasha's, hiding a story that doesn't exist, `archive()`, and reading multiple scan pages. All 10 tests pass on the fix and all 10 fail on the old code.\n\n**One thing to check:** the repo's fake table and notes say a bare reserved word in a field list is silently dropped. As far as I know, AWS documents it as an error instead. If that's right, the 200 on staging means something in `api.py`, which isn't in this repo, is hiding an error. The fix removes the bare names either way, but that handler is worth a look.\n\nAfter deploying, hide `why-i-left-mobx` on prod again, since both earlier attempts failed. `my-first-week` on staging should leave the feed once the new build is live, with no data fix needed. I haven't committed or deployed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":486,"cache_read_input_tokens":35093,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":486},"output_tokens":692,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"208e1dc4-7346-4c90-9173-38e71cdbff78","uuid":"7a10fac1-42bf-466f-a8b3-b51f6f699eb7","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:54:01.020Z","request_id":"req_011CgEPbP4xTvxD2diyLrFG2"}
